  "overview": {
    "text": "A glacier is a long-lasting mass of land ice—often mixed with snow, rock, and sediment—that forms where snowfall builds up faster than it melts. Gravity makes the ice flow downhill: ice crystals deform internally, and the glacier may also slide over or shear its bed. Debris trapped in the ice grinds and plucks rock, carving features such as U-shaped valleys and cirques. As ice melts, it leaves debris, including ridges called moraines, while meltwater can carry and sort sediment farther away. A glacier’s end can advance or retreat as its gains and losses change, but the ice continues to flow. Glaciers reshape terrain and store freshwater.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/glaciers-and-icecaps",
      "https://home.nps.gov/articles/howglaciersmove.htm",
      "https://www.nps.gov/articles/howglacierchangethelandscape.htm"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Glaciers form where snow accumulation exceeds melting; accumulated snow is compressed into ice.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/glaciers-and-icecaps"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Glacier movement combines deformation within the ice with motion at its base, such as sliding or shearing sediment.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://home.nps.gov/articles/howglaciersmove.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Abrasion grinds and scratches bedrock; plucking removes rock chunks that the ice can carry.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/howglacierchangethelandscape.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Moraines are piles of sediment deposited by glaciers; meltwater can transport and rework sediment as outwash.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/articles/howglacierchangethelandscape.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A glacier’s terminus may retreat while its ice keeps flowing downhill; retreat happens when melting exceeds accumulation.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nps.gov/glba/learn/nature/anatomy-of-a-glacier.htm"
      ]
    }
  ],
